    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">How Mortgage Lenders Define Self-Employment and Why Independent Contractors Need to Understand It Before They Apply</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you work as an independent contractor or consultant and you've started exploring mortgage options, you've probably already sensed that the process is going to be more complicated than it is for a salaried colleague. The complication starts earlier than most people expect, before income documentation, before rate quotes. It starts with how the lender classifies you.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Classification That Changes Everything</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Mortgage lenders define self-employment in a specific and consequential way. If you own 25% or more of a business, or if you receive income reported on a Schedule C, Schedule E, Schedule F, or as a partner in a partnership, you are considered self-employed for mortgage qualification purposes regardless of how you think of yourself professionally. Many contractors who consider themselves employees of their own practice, or who work under a single long-term contract that feels like a job, are classified as self-employed the moment the underwriter reviews their tax returns. That classification triggers a different set of documentation requirements and income calculation methods.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Two-Year Rule and What It Actually Means</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Conventional lenders require a minimum of two years of self-employment history before they will count that income toward qualification at all. Two years is not a guideline with flexibility. It is a hard threshold for most conventional programs.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The two years are measured from the date on your most recently filed tax return showing self-employment income. If you filed your most recent return showing one year of contractor income, you don't meet the threshold regardless of how long you've actually been contracting. If you recently transitioned from W-2 employment to contract work, you may be in a waiting period that has nothing to do with your income level or your ability to make a mortgage payment. Bank statement loans, 1099 loans, and P&L loans each have different seasoning requirements that can bridge that gap, some with as little as 12 months of documented self-employment income.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Income Is Calculated Once You Clear the Two-Year Threshold</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Meeting the two-year requirement doesn't mean your full income counts. Conventional lenders calculate self-employment qualifying income by taking your net income from Schedule C, adding back depreciation and depletion, and averaging the result over 24 months. Business expenses, home office deductions, vehicle costs, professional development, equipment, all of it reduces the number the lender works from.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For consultants and contractors whose actual overhead is low relative to revenue, the deductions that reduce their tax bill create a significant gap between what they earn and what the lender will count.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Determine how your income is reported on your tax returns before you apply anywhere.</strong> If it appears on a Schedule C or as partnership income, you are self-employed under mortgage guidelines regardless of how your work is structured.</li>
      <li><strong>Count your qualifying months carefully.</strong> Two full years of self-employment income must appear across your filed tax returns. Confirm you meet that threshold before you invest time in a conventional application.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate your actual conventional qualifying income.</strong> Take your Schedule C net income from the last two years, add back depreciation and depletion, and divide by 24. That monthly figure is what a conventional lender will use.</li>
      <li><strong>If you don't yet have two years of self-employment history, identify your Non-QM options.</strong> Bank statement loans, 1099 loans, and P&L loans each have different seasoning requirements. Some can work with as little as 12 months of documented self-employment income.</li>
      <li><strong>Talk to a lender who regularly works with self-employed borrowers before you apply.</strong> The classification rules and income calculation methods are specific enough that experience with this borrower type matters significantly in how your application is structured.</li>
    </ol>

